    {
      "id": "sutr-AZ7UXnOcWPrnzaiv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3730.5314853695972,
      "y": 590.3280376342393,
      "width": 131.99981689453125,
      "height": 60,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ak",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 279825747,
      "version": 424,
      "versionNonce": 2107025756,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784484186034,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ChatParticipant\n- chatId\n- participantId",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "ChatParticipant\n- chatId\n- participantId",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HIt-9TXR1xxidmbDOoPCI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3932.6032113782485,
      "y": 580.312990025207,
      "width": 496.585179310478,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "al",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1048885725,
      "version": 610,
      "versionNonce": 808525276,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784484186034,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "On the chat table, we'll usually just want to look up\nthe details by the chat's ID. Having a simple primary\nkey on the chat id is good enough for this.\n\nFor the ChatParticipant table, we'll want to be able to\n(1) look up all participants for a given chat and (2)\nlook up all chats for a given user.\n\n(1) We can do this with a composite primary key where\nchatId is the partition key (PK) and participantId is\nthe sort key (SK). A Query on the chatId partition key\nwill return all participants for a given chat.\n\n(2) We'll need a Global Secondary Index (GSI) with\nparticipantId as the partition key (PK) and chatId as\nthe sort key (SK). This will allow us to efficiently\nquery all chats for a given user. The GSI will\nautomatically be kept in sync with the base table by\nDynamoDB.\nGreat! We got some chats. How about messages?",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "On the chat table, we'll usually just want to look up the details by the chat's ID. Having a simple primary key on the chat id is good enough for this.\n\nFor the ChatParticipant table, we'll want to be able to (1) look up all participants for a given chat and (2) look up all chats for a given user.\n\n(1) We can do this with a composite primary key where chatId is the partition key (PK) and participantId is the sort key (SK). A Query on the chatId partition key will return all participants for a given chat.\n\n(2) We'll need a Global Secondary Index (GSI) with participantId as the partition key (PK) and chatId as the sort key (SK). This will allow us to efficiently query all chats for a given user. The GSI will automatically be kept in sync with the base table by DynamoDB.\nGreat! We got some chats. How about messages?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "l-1AV1WroQCEnwZWSxxdf",
