    {
      "id": "uhfBFT1FrXkgjZHVmnQpR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6655.566051759571,
      "y": 1759.4930684671963,
      "width": 793.1313748070579,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1Y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 553237165,
      "version": 653,
      "versionNonce": 2022935260,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784482682218,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Challenges\n\n-> Each Chat Server will need to maintain connections with each other Chat\nServer which will require that we keep our Chat Servers big in size and small in\nnumber.\n\n-> Increasing the number of Chat Servers requires careful orchestration of\ndropping connections so that users reconnect to other servers without\ntriggering a thundering herd (we need to be able to support users moving\nbetween servers). \n\n-> During scaling we need to ensure that events are sent to both servers to\nprevent dropping messages.\n\n-> All of these are solvable problems but your interviewer will expect you to talk\nabout them and how to pull it off. \n\nRead Realtime Updates Pattern\n\n\nGOOD Soln. Interviewer will expect you to talk about these",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Challenges\n\n-> Each Chat Server will need to maintain connections with each other Chat Server which will require that we keep our Chat Servers big in size and small in number.\n\n-> Increasing the number of Chat Servers requires careful orchestration of dropping connections so that users reconnect to other servers without triggering a thundering herd (we need to be able to support users moving between servers). \n\n-> During scaling we need to ensure that events are sent to both servers to prevent dropping messages.\n\n-> All of these are solvable problems but your interviewer will expect you to talk about them and how to pull it off. \n\nRead Realtime Updates Pattern\n\n\nGOOD Soln. Interviewer will expect you to talk about these",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "cVWpso4yM1IMkSbspROvv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6644.510120449409,
      "y": 1244.208260089968,
      "width": 772.2475144266579,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1Z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2111554733,
      "version": 361,
      "versionNonce": 1276497756,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784482682218,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Challenges\n\nThis unfortunately doesn't work - Kafka is not built for billions of topics and\ncarries significant overhead for each one (order of 50kb per topic, so 50tb+\nof storage for 1b users).\n\nBAD Soln",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Challenges\n\nThis unfortunately doesn't work - Kafka is not built for billions of topics and carries significant overhead for each one (order of 50kb per topic, so 50tb+ of storage for 1b users).\n\nBAD Soln",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "JYzwRj1Cz3dmyE_ZzOMoE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4771.612619000098,
      "y": 2306.478070918814,
      "width": 77,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1a",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1575753699,
      "version": 427,
      "versionNonce": 1876657116,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784482682218,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Pub/Sub",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Pub/Sub",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "bcXtfhkFxuyBVqwHuRQJZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5606.8721817827545,
      "y": 2313.151220635145,
      "width": 941.0214833607924,
      "height": 875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 428275907,
      "version": 794,
      "versionNonce": 375868004,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784482701249,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Redis Pub/Sub is a good example which uses a very lightweight hashmap of socket\nconnections to allow you to ferry messages. With Pub/Sub you can create a\nsubscription for a given user ID and then publish messages to that subscription which\nare received \"at most once\" by the subscribers.\nThe difference here with Kafka is that Pub/Sub is not managing storage of messages.\nKafka requires significant disk-based overhead per topic (in addition to the messages\nthemselves) since it persists messages to disk and maintains consumer offsets. Redis\nPub/Sub channels are much lighter — they're essentially in-memory pointers to\nsubscriber connections with NO MESSAGE PERSISTENCE. \n\nNo single Redis instance. Shard across a cluster of Redis instances (partitioning\nchannels by user ID), with each instance handling a fraction of the total channels.\n\nOn connection:\nWhen users connect to our Chat Server, that server will connect to Pub/Sub to\nsubscribe to the topic for that user ID.\nAny messages received on that subscription are then forwarded on to the websocket\nconnection for that user.\n\nWhen a message needs to be sent:\nWe publish the message to the Pub/Sub topic for the relevant user ID.\nThe message is received by all subscribing Chat Servers.\n\nThose Chat Servers then forward the message to the user's websocket connection.\nPub/Sub is \"at most once\" which means it doesn't guarantee delivery. If there's no\nsubscribers listening or Redis has a transient failure, that message is lost.\n\nThis is acceptable because we write to the Inbox and Message tables before publishing\nto Pub/Sub. The write path is:\n1. Write message to Message table + create Inbox entries (durable)\n2. Return success to sender\n3. Publish to Pub/Sub for real-time delivery (best-effort)\nIf step 3 fails, the message is still durably stored. Recipients will receive it when\nthey reconnect (via the Inbox sync described above) or through periodic polling for\nconnected clients that missed a pub/sub message.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Redis Pub/Sub is a good example which uses a very lightweight hashmap of socket connections to allow you to ferry messages. With Pub/Sub you can create a subscription for a given user ID and then publish messages to that subscription which are received \"at most once\" by the subscribers.\nThe difference here with Kafka is that Pub/Sub is not managing storage of messages. Kafka requires significant disk-based overhead per topic (in addition to the messages themselves) since it persists messages to disk and maintains consumer offsets. Redis Pub/Sub channels are much lighter — they're essentially in-memory pointers to subscriber connections with NO MESSAGE PERSISTENCE. \n\nNo single Redis instance. Shard across a cluster of Redis instances (partitioning channels by user ID), with each instance handling a fraction of the total channels.\n\nOn connection:\nWhen users connect to our Chat Server, that server will connect to Pub/Sub to subscribe to the topic for that user ID.\nAny messages received on that subscription are then forwarded on to the websocket connection for that user.\n\nWhen a message needs to be sent:\nWe publish the message to the Pub/Sub topic for the relevant user ID.\nThe message is received by all subscribing Chat Servers.\n\nThose Chat Servers then forward the message to the user's websocket connection.\nPub/Sub is \"at most once\" which means it doesn't guarantee delivery. If there's no subscribers listening or Redis has a transient failure, that message is lost.\n\nThis is acceptable because we write to the Inbox and Message tables before publishing to Pub/Sub. The write path is:\n1. Write message to Message table + create Inbox entries (durable)\n2. Return success to sender\n3. Publish to Pub/Sub for real-time delivery (best-effort)\nIf step 3 fails, the message is still durably stored. Recipients will receive it when they reconnect (via the Inbox sync described above) or through periodic polling for connected clients that missed a pub/sub message.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "89-VqOukplKJEZxeo-DBl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6662.6605853785,
      "y": 2378.599720671275,
      "width": 793.1313748070579,
      "height": 275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1w",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1661365155,
      "version": 732,
      "versionNonce": 1777772636,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784482682218,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Challenges\n\n-> The Pub/Sub implementation introduces additional latency because we need\nto ferry messages via Redis. This is small (single-digit milliseconds) but it's still\na cost.\n\n-> We also have connections required between each Chat Server and each Redis\ncluster server. This is surmountable because the number of instances we'll need\nis relatively small.\n\nBEST",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Challenges\n\n-> The Pub/Sub implementation introduces additional latency because we need to ferry messages via Redis. This is small (single-digit milliseconds) but it's still a cost.\n\n-> We also have connections required between each Chat Server and each Redis cluster server. This is surmountable because the number of instances we'll need is relatively small.\n\nBEST",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
